  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry but cloudy today. Rain on Sunday, then drying out \nfor early next week.\n\nDry weather is expected today, but cloud cover will continue to\nincrease across the area, ahead of a system developing over the\nsoutheastern US. Models are in agreement in an area of low \npressure forming along a front currently analyzed over the FL \npeninsula as it moves north along the East Coast. There is \nbetter consensus now that the center of the low will remain near\nthe coast but offshore, and bring some rain to the region late \ntonight, though mostly on Sunday. The airmass residing over the \nMid Atlantic is very dry, PWAT on the 00Z RNK sounding just over\na quarter inch, so will take some time for the atmospheric \ncolumn to saturate enough for rain. Considerable spread in \npotential rainfall amounts persists, but expected amounts are \naround half to three-quarters of an inch from the Blue Ridge \nFoothills and east. The high end amount, or 10% chance of \nexceedance, is up to 2.5 inches or so in those same areas, so\ncannot rule out higher totals. Any rain in this region would be\nbeneficial, given the persistent Severe to Extreme drought \nconditions across southern VA and central NC. Could see some \nrunoff within any heavier embedded showers, and possible \noverflowing of leaf clogged ditches or drainage areas, but the\npotential for widespread flooding is low. Will likely be light \nrain beginning early Sunday morning, then increasing in coverage\nby sunrise and continuing past sunset. \n\nThe upper trough moving towards the area will help to drag the \nsurface low northward late in the weekend, and will bring additional \nchances for precipitation to the west with the passage of a cold \nfront. However, as the upper energy pulls farther to the north, \nthinking the rain will fade as it gets to the mountains. High \npressure will build across the northeast behind this system by early \nnext week, wedging down the eastern side of the Appalachians by the \nmiddle of the week, keeping it dry but cooler and breezy through\nTuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Precipitation chances increasing for Wednesday \nthrough Thanksgiving.\n\nBy the middle of the week, a 500mb trough deepens over the \ncentral US and tracks eastward, while another frontal system \ndevelops over the Gulf states. The trough looks to eventually \nlead to more cyclogenesis as it interacts with the southern \nstream system, and move towards the Mid Atlantic and Northeast. \nThis will likely bring some precipitation to the area during or \nnear Thanksgiving. Low level cold air in place ahead of this \nsystem suggests some degree of mixed precipitation possible, but\nit is too far out in the forecast period and there is too much \nvariability among the guidance to say with great confidence if \nand how much freezing rain or snow we will see with this system.\nMost likely, any wintry precipitation will be confined to the \nhigher elevations in the west, with more rain in the east. \nDespite the uncertainty in specific timing and extent of \nimpacts, given the proximity of this system to major travel days\naround Thanksgiving, we encourage the public to stay up to date\nwith the holiday forecast.",
